[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Divide and Conquer Sorting: Merge Sort and Quick Sort"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Divide and Conquer is a powerful algorithmic paradigm that solves a problem by recursively breaking it down into smaller subproblems of the same type, until these become simple enough to solve directly. The solutions to the subproblems are then combined to give a solution to the original problem. This tutorial explores two prominent sorting algorithms based on this paradigm: Merge Sort and Quick Sort."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Merge Sort"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Merge Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that follows the divide and conquer approach. It recursively divides the input array into two halves, sorts each half, and then merges the sorted halves."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Divide: Divide the unsorted list into n sublists, each containing one element (a list of one element is considered sorted)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conquer: Repeatedly merge sublists to produce new sorted sublists until there is only one sublist remaining. This will be the sorted list."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) {
    return arr;
  }

  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = arr.slice(0, mid);
  const right = arr.slice(mid);

  return merge(mergeSort(left), mergeSort(right));
}

function merge(left, right) {
  let result = [];
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] < right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i]);
      i++;
    } else {
      result.push(right[j]);
      j++;
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'mergeSort' function recursively divides the array until it reaches single-element arrays. The 'merge' function then combines these sorted arrays into larger sorted arrays until the entire array is sorted.  Merge sort guarantees a time complexity of O(n log n) in all cases."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Quick Sort"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Quick Sort is also a divide and conquer algorithm, but unlike Merge Sort, it performs the sorting in place. It works by selecting a 'pivot' element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Divide: Choose a pivot element from the array."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Partition: Rearrange the array so that all elements less than the pivot are placed before it, and all elements greater than the pivot are placed after it. Equal values can go either way. After this partitioning, the pivot is in its final position."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Conquer: Recursively apply the above steps to the sub-array of elements with smaller values and separately to the sub-array of elements with greater values."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"function quickSort(arr, low, high) {
  if (low < high) {
    let pi = partition(arr, low, high);

    quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
    quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
  }
}

function partition(arr, low, high) {
  let pivot = arr[high];
  let i = (low - 1);

  for (let j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      swap(arr, i, j);
    }
  }
  swap(arr, i + 1, high);
  return (i + 1);
}

function swap(arr, i, j) {
  let temp = arr[i];
  arr[i] = arr[j];
  arr[j] = temp;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'quickSort' function recursively sorts the array by partitioning it around a pivot. The 'partition' function rearranges the array so that elements smaller than the pivot are before it, and elements larger are after it. The 'swap' function is a helper function to exchange elements. Quick sort has an average time complexity of O(n log n), but in the worst case (e.g., when the pivot is always the smallest or largest element), it can degrade to O(n^2)."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing a good pivot is crucial for Quick Sort's performance. Common strategies include choosing the first element, the last element, a random element, or the median of three elements."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Merge Sort: Guaranteed O(n log n) time complexity, but requires extra space for merging."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quick Sort: Average O(n log n) time complexity, but worst-case O(n^2). Generally faster in practice than Merge Sort due to lower constant factors, and sorts in place (no extra space required, although the recursive calls use stack space)."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between Merge Sort and Quick Sort depends on the specific application. If guaranteed performance is critical and extra space is acceptable, Merge Sort is a good choice. If space is a constraint and average-case performance is sufficient, Quick Sort is often preferred."}]
